title: The command input
tags: Commands
kind: guide
aliases: command-input minibuffer editing autocomplete suggestions
summary: The line above the status bar: type a command after `:` or a shell line after `!`, pick from the suggestions, answer what a command or a script asks.

The command input is the line just above the status bar, always there. It runs
[[commands|Commands]], runs shell lines, and is where a command or a script asks
its questions.

* <<key "command-input:focus command" ":">> focuses it in ''command mode'' — the prompt reads `:`.
* <<key "command-input:focus bash" "!">> focuses it in ''bash mode'' — the prompt reads `!` ([[Bash mode]]). Typing `!` first in an empty command line switches too.
* <<key "editing:unfocus" Escape>> leaves it, keeping what was typed.

There is one command input, and it acts on the workspace of the focused slot. What
is typed is kept ''per workspace and per mode'': moving to another workspace brings
back its own half-typed line, and each mode has its own.

!! Running a command

Type some of a command's name. The list above the line shows the commands that
match, with what each does and the keys bound to it; it shows only while the input
has the focus.

* The match is by ordered pieces, not by prefix: the text is split at spaces and the pieces must appear in the name in that order, in any case. `con def` matches like `.*con.*def.*`. Names starting with the first piece come first.
* The first row is highlighted. `Up` and `Down` move the highlight, `Home` and `End` jump to the ends.
* <<key "editing:confirm" Enter>> ''runs the highlighted row'' — so `pa sw` then `Enter` runs `panel:swap`. When nothing is listed, or when the line already starts with a command's whole name, it runs the line as typed (below).
* `Tab` writes the highlighted row into the line without running it, ready for arguments.

A row ending in `…` is a command that will ask for something before acting
([[Interactive command arguments]]); one without acts at once. The `…` is not part of
the name.

The list holds every command, and one more row for each ''key bound to an invocation
with arguments'': `panel:open here file` has its row, with its key, because a key runs
it. So every shortcut can be found by reading the list, and the variants nobody bound
do not fill it — they are reached by running the command bare and answering.

A line whose first word is already a command's whole name is run ''as typed'', whatever
the list shows: `panel:open here` then `Enter` asks for the type, it does not run the
first `panel:open here …` listed. Moving the highlight is a choice, and then the
highlighted row runs.

!! Editing keys

They act on the command input, and on any text field of a panel that has the focus:

|!Key |!Command |!Does |
|<<key "editing:confirm" Enter>> |`editing:confirm` |submit — the command input only; in a panel's field the key is the panel's |
|<<key "editing:unfocus" Escape>> |`editing:unfocus` |leave, keeping the text |
|<<key "editing:discard" "ctrl+q">> |`editing:discard` |empty the field and leave |
|<<key "editing:goto line-start" "ctrl+a">> |`editing:goto line-start` |caret to the start |
|<<key "editing:goto line-end" "ctrl+e">> |`editing:goto line-end` |caret to the end |

`ctrl+p`, `ctrl+n` and `ctrl+r` recall what was typed before ([[Input history]]).

!! Answering a question

When a command needs an argument, or a script asks something, the prompt shows the
question in place of `:` and the list shows the possible answers. The keys differ
slightly from command mode — see [[Interactive command arguments]], and [[Script sessions]] for a script's questions. `Escape` gives up.

A question belongs to the workspace it was asked in. Showing another workspace puts
it away — the line is an ordinary command line again — and coming back restores it,
with what was typed.
